{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled from 1155 to 1194 a significant period in the history of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Levant. He was a key figure in maintaining the county's independence navigating complex political landscapes and engaging in frequent warfare with neighboring powers including the Seljuk Turks and the burgeoning Crusader kingdoms.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III inherited the County of Tripoli from his father Raymond II in 1155. His early reign was marked by challenges including Seljuk incursions and internal power struggles. He demonstrated shrewd political maneuvering and military skill to consolidate his authority. He faced opposition from various factions within Tripoli and from external threats seeking to diminish its power. Raymond III's early actions focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and securing its borders. He also worked to maintain alliances with other Crusader states particularly Antioch and Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed significant military campaigns against the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum. He launched numerous expeditions to raid Seljuk territory aiming to disrupt their military strength and extract tribute. These campaigns were often costly but generally successful in maintaining a precarious balance of power. He also engaged in conflicts with other Crusader rulers often vying for influence and territory in the region. His military prowess earned him a reputation as a formidable warrior and a skilled military leader. He understood the importance of both offensive and defensive strategies to protect Tripoli's interests.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* Raymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at forging and maintaining alliances. He cultivated relationships with various powers including the Holy Roman Empire the Kingdom of Sicily and other Crusader states. These alliances provided crucial support against common enemies and helped to secure Tripoli's position in the region. He understood the importance of balancing relationships to avoid becoming overly reliant on any single power. His diplomatic efforts were crucial to the survival and prosperity of the County of Tripoli. He often used marriage alliances to strengthen his political position further solidifying ties with other influential families and states.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy* In his later years Raymond III faced increasing challenges from the expanding power of the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin. He continued to resist Ayyubid advances but ultimately could not prevent the loss of Tripoli to Saladin in 1183. Despite the loss of the county Raymond III left a lasting legacy as a capable ruler and a staunch defender of the Crusader cause. He is remembered for his military skill diplomatic acumen and dedication to protecting Tripoli's independence. His reign represents a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli a period of relative stability and prosperity amidst ongoing conflict. He is considered one of the most important Counts of Tripoli ensuring its survival for a significant period against overwhelming odds.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -1.7221109867095947}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -3.886147169396281e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.169585604860913e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.08634165674448013}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.08379924297332764}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.0001658063702052459}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.7762025890988298e-05}, {"token": "9", "